, particularly in the United States and Europe."}], "places": [{"name": "Los Angeles, USA", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "New York City, USA", "role": "Major Scene"}, {"name": "London, UK", "role": "Influential Hub"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: metalcore", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "metalcore"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q183862", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metalcore"], "tags": ["music genre", "Music & Compositions", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "metalcore contributed to the historical record of the Contemporary period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Metalcore significantly shaped the music landscape of the late 20th and early 21st centuries, influencing a generation of musicians and establishing a new cultural movement that continues to thrive today.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-10T01:34:55Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27246933637", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Fusion genre of heavy metal and hardcore punk Created c. 1988.", "newValue": "Metalcore, a fusion genre combining elements of heavy metal and hardcore punk, emerged in the late 1980s, roughly around 1988, primarily in the United States. This genre represents a significant evolution in the music landscape, offering a raw, intense sound characterized by aggressive guitar riffs, breakdowns, and a blend of melodic and harsh vocal styles. Key bands such as Earth Crisis, Hatebreed, and Converge have been pivotal in defining this genre, each bringing their distinctive influences and innovations to the table. Metalcore's global reach has transformed it into a prominent genre, impacting the development of various subgenres and influencing countless musicians worldwide.

The birth of metalcore is marked by the integration of metal's technical prowess with the emotional and social elements of hardcore punk. During the 1990s and into the early 2000s, bands like Killswitch Engage and All That Remains popularized the genre, leading to a surge in metalcore's mainstream acceptance. Metalcore festivals, such as the annual Warped Tour, showcased a multitude of bands, effectively creating a community around this music style. By the mid-2000s, metalcore had become synonymous with an entire generation's musical identity, with albums like "Alive or Just Breathing" (2002) by Killswitch Engage achieving critical acclaim and commercial success.

